Maintainer wish, 2026-11-24, found while planning k256 (topic agents that pre-load skills). Owner: unreagent-skill-author (skills/).

Measured at e47205b: 20 of the 30 shipped skills are a single SKILL.md of 10 to 32 KB with no section files; the heaviest are ue-assets (31.9 KB), unreagent-iterate (30.8 KB), ue-audio (24.4 KB), ue-level-actors (22.2 KB), ue-lighting (21.5 KB), unreagent-files (20.8 KB), ue-control-rig (20.2 KB), ue-sequencer (19.8 KB). A pre-loaded SKILL.md stays in a subagent context for its whole run (k256), and the `skills` MCP tool returns the whole body too.

Maintainer wording: split generously, more section files and more references are always good, BUT not so thin that the section is not loaded when it is needed.

What done looks like, per skill:
- SKILL.md keeps what every use needs: the decision rules, the facts that must not be got wrong, the common path. An agent that read only SKILL.md does the common task correctly.
- Reference material, long tables, rare paths and troubleshooting go into `<skill>/<section>.md`.
- For each section, SKILL.md says what is in it and exactly when to load it (a condition, not "see also").
- No content is lost or reworded into something unverified: this is a move, every claim keeps its source label. The toolkit lint and `go test ./internal/skills` stay green.

Order: the heaviest single-block skills first; ue-context (shared on all agents) and ue-assets early, since k256 pre-loads them. Skills that already have sections are checked against the same rule and split further where a block is still heavy. Work in batches of a few skills per review so a reviewer can compare before and after.

Not in scope: new content, the project own .claude/skills/unreagent-* documents.

